# The Gradle wrapper IS committed here, unlike in Flutter's stock template, which
# ignores `gradle-wrapper.jar`, `/gradlew` and `/gradlew.bat`. That default is fine
# for a project where Gradle is only ever invoked through `flutter build`, which
# finds Gradle itself and never needs the script. It is wrong for this one: CI runs
# `./gradlew :opendash_dash_engine:testDebugUnitTest` and `lintDebug` directly
# (see .github/workflows/nightly.yml), and with the wrapper ignored those steps died
# with `./gradlew: No such file or directory` on every run from 2026-09-11 to 09-14 —
# four nightlies and every release, because release.yml carries the same two lines.
#
# Committing it is also what Gradle intends: the wrapper exists precisely so a
# checkout builds with a pinned Gradle and no preinstalled toolchain.
#
# What is verified, and what is not. `gradle/actions/wrapper-validation` in both
# workflows checksums `gradle-wrapper.jar` alone against Gradle's published list,
# and `distributionSha256Sum` in gradle-wrapper.properties pins the 232 MB
# distribution the jar then downloads and executes. Neither covers `gradlew` or
# `gradlew.bat`, which are ordinary committed shell/batch scripts that CI runs —
# they are reviewed like any other code, not checksummed. The API-key step in both
# workflows was moved to sit after the Gradle steps for that reason.
/.gradle
/captures/
/local.properties
/dart_defines.local.properties
/maptiler.local.properties
GeneratedPluginRegistrant.java
.cxx/

# Remember to never publicly share your keystore.
# See https://flutter.dev/to/reference-keystore
key.properties
**/*.keystore
**/*.jks
